From b3682a2c90afe3e89bf67eacde9b59257fc9f4ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 03:35:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Deliver to-device messages through the durable outbox
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous commit fired an m.direct_to_device EDU at the peer and forgot
it. On a Bluetooth mesh that is the wrong shape: the peer you are sharing
a Megolm room key with is a phone that walks in and out of range, and a
key dropped because it was out of range for a second is a conversation
that cannot be read.

EDUs now get a row in the federation outbox — a new `outbox_edus` table,
stored verbatim because an EDU has no event id to reference — and the
per-destination sender carries them in the same transaction as any
pending PDUs, or one of their own when nothing else is queued. A
destination owed only a room key gets a sender task; an unreachable peer
is retried with the usual backoff and kicked when connectivity returns;
the rows go only on the peer's terminal status. The row is keyed on the
client's /sendToDevice transaction id, so a client retry queues nothing
new, and the same id travels as `message_id` for the peer's own dedup.

FederationTransport::send_transaction gains an `edus` argument; the
best-effort `send_edu` path is gone. Schema version bumps to 3.

Tests: EDU-only delivery and drain, EDUs riding along with PDUs, and the
one that is the point — a key queued while the peer is unreachable is
delivered when the peer comes back. Plus the store's round-trip,
idempotency and watch-wake tests, and an HTTP test that /sendToDevice to a
remote user lands in the outbox once however many times it is retried.

CLAUDE.md and docs/federation-memberships.md now say what is implemented:
the server's half of E2EE, and m.direct_to_device among the EDUs.
